<worksheet>
<title>Phase Portraits for linear systems with repeated eigenvalues, and a zero eigenvalue
</title>
<objectives>
<ul>
            <li>Phase Portraits for linear systems with repeated eigenvalues.</li>
            
                <li>Phase Portraits for linear systems with a zero eigenvalue.</li>
        </ul>    
</objectives>

<introduction>
    <p>
       In this worksheet we will discuss the cases of the phase portraits for the linear
               system
               <md>
                   \frac{d\vec{Y}}{dt}=A\vec{Y}
               </md>
               where <m>A</m> is a <m>2\times 2</m> matrix over <m>\mathbb{R}</m>, with
               repeated and zero eigenvalues of <m>A</m>. 
    </p>
</introduction>
<section>
    <title>A system with repeated eigenvalue</title>
    

    <theorem>
        <statement>
            <p>
               Suppose that <m>\displaystyle{\frac{d\vec{Y}}{dt}=A\vec{Y}}</m> is a linear
                system in which the <m>2\times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> has a repeated real
                 eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> but only one line of eigenvectors.
                 Then the general solution has the form
                 <md>
                \vec{Y}(t)=e^{\lambda t}\vec{V_0}+te^{\lambda t}\vec{V_1},</md>
             where <m>\vec{V_0}=(x_{0},y_{0})</m> is an arbitrary initial condition and
              <md>
             \vec{V_1}=(A-\lambda I)\vec{V_0}. </md>
            If <m>\vec{V_1}</m> is zero, then the previous equation implies that
             <m>\vec{V_0}</m> is an eigenvector of <m>A</m> and <m>\vec{Y}(t)</m> is a straight-line solution.
            Otherwise <m>\vec{V_1}</m> is an eigenvector of <m>A</m>. 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </theorem>
    <example>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                
            For the system
             <md alignment="align">
            <mrow>\frac{dx}{dt}&amp;=&amp;2x+y</mrow>
          <mrow>\frac{dy}{dt}&amp;=&amp;-x+4y</mrow>
                        </md>
       
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Find the eigenvalue.
 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Find the eigenvectors.
 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Sketch the direction field.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Sketch the phase portrait, including the solution curve with initial condition
                                       <m>\vec{Y}(0) = (1, 0)</m>; and 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Sketch the <m>x(t)</m>- and <m>y(t)</m>-graphs of the solution with initial
                                       condition <m>\vec{Y}(0) = (1, 0)</m>. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </example>
</section>

<section>
    <title>A system with zero Eigenvalue</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
           Suppose that <m>\displaystyle{\frac{d\vec{Y}}{dt}=A\vec{Y}}</m> is a linear
                       system in which the <m>2\times 2</m> matrix <m>A</m> has a zero and another real
                       eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m> with corresponding eigenvectors <m>\vec{V_1}</m> and
                       <m>\vec{V_2}</m>.
                       Then the general solution has the form
                       <md>
                           \vec{Y}(t)=c_{1}\vec{V_1}+c_{2}e^{\lambda t}\vec{V_2}.
                       </md>
                       Notice that the stability of the solution (source or sink) depends on whether
                       <m>\lambda</m> is positive of negative.
                       The solution will be lines parallel to <m>\vec{V_2}</m> that will sink or source
                       from the line <m>\vec{V_1}</m>. 
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <example>
        <introduction>
            <p>
               For the system
                               <md alignment="align">
                                   <mrow>\frac{dx}{dt}&amp;=&amp;4x+2y</mrow>
                                   <mrow>\frac{dy}{dt}&amp;=&amp;2x+y</mrow>
                               </md> 
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the eigenvalues.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the eigenvectors.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Sketch the phase portrait, including the solution curve with initial condition
                                       <m>\vec{Y}(0) = (1, 0)</m>; and 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                   Find the general solution.
                                       Find the particular solution with <m>\vec{Y}(0) = (1, 0)</m> compare it with
                                       your sketch from part (c). 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </example>
</section>
</worksheet>